  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 209 PM EDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast: Mid-upper level heights continue to rise \nabove our heads this afternoon as troughing largely ejects off the \nEast Coast...save for an upper low that cuts off and is progged to \nspin off the coast of VA/NC for much of the week ahead. Early this \nweek, another upper low is expected to be diving south off the West \nCoast, making very little eastward progress until later this week. \nAll this setting the stage for developing Omega blocking across the \nCONUS this week, with a zonal to weakly ridged upper-level pattern \ndominating locally. Surface high pressure dominates locally with an \nactive northern stream across northern Canada just simply too far \nremoved from having much of an impact on our sensible weather.\n\nForecast Details: Continued focus over the near-term revolves around \ncontinued windy conditions, especially at and near the Lake Michigan \nshorelines into the Straits area. 40+ mph gusts remain most likely \nin these area, with some 45+ mph gusts not out of the question. \nThose higher end gusts not likely to be widespread or frequent \nenough to warrant a wind advisory, but it's close. 25-35 mph common \nelsewhere through this afternoon before gradually diminishing this \nevening, and especially tonight.\n\nOtherwise, remarkably quiet weather on tap for much of the week \nahead, with Thursday's potential brief shower chances largely out of \nplay at this point. Next rain chance likely to hold off until next \nweekend, if not beyond. High temps for much of the week ahead \nvarying through the 60s with a few spots in northern lower \npotentially making a run to near 70/into the lower 70s.",
